# Flow Language

The Flow language describes a graph of named Action calls and one return
expression. Its primitives are data. They do not run Elixir code while the
Flow is authored.

The Elixir module DSL, Flow Script, and `Jido.Flow.Builder` all lower to the
same canonical `%Jido.Flow{}` artifact. This notebook gives a short tour of
the common language. The focused guides explain each primitive in more depth.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0"}])
```

## Define Small Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Double do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_double",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{value: value * 2}}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Add do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_language_add",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer(), value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{total: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{total: total, value: value}, _context), do: {:ok, %{total: total + value}}
end
```

## `step`: Call An Action

`step` adds one named node. The `with:` expression maps Flow data to Action
input.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.StepExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_step",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    echoed = step(:echo, FlowLanguage.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})
    return(echoed)
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 7}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.StepExample, %{value: 7}, %{})
```

## `group` And `branch`: Organise Static Branches

`group` contains named `branch` blocks. The group and branch names become
provenance. Dependencies still come from references and `after:`. The group
does not enable concurrent execution.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.GroupExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_group",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{left: Zoi.integer(), right: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    group do
      branch :left_side do
        left_result = step(:left, FlowLanguage.Echo, with: %{value: input(:left)})
      end

      branch :right_side do
        right_result = step(:right, FlowLanguage.Echo, with: %{value: input(:right)})
      end
    end

    merged =
      step(:merge, FlowLanguage.Echo,
        with: %{left: select(left_result, :value), right: select(right_result, :value)}
      )

    return(merged)
  end
end

{:ok, %{left: 1, right: 2}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.GroupExample, %{left: 1, right: 2}, %{})
```

## `map` And `reduce`: Process Collections

`map` calls one target for each item in a proper list. Its `with:` expression
can use `item`, `item_index`, and `item_id`. `reduce` performs a serial left
fold. Its `with:` expression can also use the current `accumulator`.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.CollectionExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_collections",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{values: Zoi.list(Zoi.integer())}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    doubled =
      map(:double_values, input(:values),
        run: FlowLanguage.Double,
        with: %{value: item()}
      )

    total =
      reduce(:sum_values, doubled,
        initial: value(%{total: 0}),
        run: FlowLanguage.Add,
        with: %{total: accumulator(:total), value: item(:value)}
      )

    return(total)
  end
end

{:ok, %{total: 12}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.CollectionExample, %{values: [1, 2, 3]}, %{})
```

Map has `:fail_fast` and `:collect_errors` modes. Reduce always runs in source
order. See [Map and Reduce](flow-collections.livemd) for result records, local
reference scope, errors, concurrency, and runtime Builder syntax.

## `loop` And State: Repeat Bounded Work

`loop` repeats one target. Its State contract defines a schema, the initial
State, and the complete State replacement after each body result. Use exactly
one `while`, `until`, or `repeat` termination form.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.LoopExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_loop",
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    counted =
      loop(:add_twice,
        run: FlowLanguage.Add,
        with: %{total: state(:total), value: value(1)},
        state: [
          schema: [],
          initial: %{total: value(0)},
          update: %{total: body_result(:total)}
        ],
        repeat: 2
      )

    return(counted)
  end
end

{:ok, %{iterations: 2, state: %{total: 2}, output: %{total: 2}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.LoopExample, %{}, %{})
```

Loop-local expressions are `state`, `iteration_index`, and `body_result`.
`while` and `until` loops also require `max_iterations`. See [Loops and
State](flow-loops-state.livemd) for completion rules, State validation, Flow
Script schema registries, and Builder syntax.

## `choose`: Select One Target

`choose` creates one Choice node. Its options are checked in authored order.
The first true condition selects a target. The required `otherwise` target is
used when no option matches.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_choice",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{kind: Zoi.atom()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    selected =
      choose :route do
        option(:special,
          when: eq(input(:kind), value(:special)),
          run: FlowLanguage.Echo,
          with: %{route: value(:special)}
        )

        otherwise(
          run: FlowLanguage.Echo,
          with: %{route: value(:default)}
        )
      end

    return(selected)
  end
end

{:ok, %{route: :special}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample, %{kind: :special}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :default}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ChoiceExample, %{kind: :other}, %{})
```

## Expressions, Bindings, And `return`

Expressions read `input`, `context`, literal `value`, or earlier `result`
values. A bound step name can be used as a result expression. `select` projects
a nested path. `return` is required and can shape a map or list from several
expressions.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.ExpressionExample do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_language_expressions",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    source_result = step(:source, FlowLanguage.Echo, with: %{value: input(:value)})

    result_value =
      step(:result, FlowLanguage.Echo,
        with: %{
          source: select(source_result, :value),
          trace_id: context(:trace_id),
          constant: value(:constant)
        }
      )

    return(%{
      value: select(result_value, :source),
      trace_id: select(result_value, :trace_id),
      constant: select(result_value, :constant)
    })
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 7, trace_id: "trace-1", constant: :constant}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.ExpressionExample, %{value: 7}, %{trace_id: "trace-1"})
```

## One Canonical Flow Artifact

The module authoring surface returns a canonical data value. The other
authoring surfaces produce the same kind of value, so inspection and execution
do not depend on how the Flow was authored.

```elixir
canonical = FlowLanguage.StepExample.flow()

true = is_struct(canonical, Jido.Flow)
%Jido.Flow{name: "flow_language_step", nodes: [_node], return: _return} = canonical

{:ok, semantic_map} = Jido.Flow.semantic_identity(canonical)
is_binary(semantic_map.digest)
```

